WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.208 --> 00:00:02.419
(host)
Vikings were some
of the fiercest fighters

00:00:02.502 --> 00:00:03.628
in human history,

00:00:03.712 --> 00:00:05.505
using their impressive
battle prowess

00:00:05.588 --> 00:00:06.923
to spread Norse influence

00:00:07.006 --> 00:00:09.467
between the 8th
and 11th centuries.

00:00:09.551 --> 00:00:11.261
Their bravery as warriors
was driven

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:15.432
by one ultimate desire:
to join Odin in Valhalla.

00:00:15.515 --> 00:00:17.142
Only the most worthy Vikings

00:00:17.225 --> 00:00:18.768
would get to spend
their afterlives

00:00:18.852 --> 00:00:19.978
in that hallowed hall,

00:00:20.061 --> 00:00:21.646
and the choice
of who made the cut

00:00:21.730 --> 00:00:25.108
fell to a group of powerful
but mysterious women.

00:00:25.191 --> 00:00:27.610
I'm talking about
the Valkyries.

00:00:27.694 --> 00:00:31.489
[intro music]

00:00:31.573 --> 00:00:33.450
Most of what we know
about the Valkyries

00:00:33.533 --> 00:00:35.660
comes from the prose
and "Poetic Eddas,"

00:00:35.744 --> 00:00:38.037
collections of mythology
and heroic tales

00:00:38.121 --> 00:00:40.540
written down
in the 13th century.

00:00:40.623 --> 00:00:42.333
In the texts,
these powerful women

00:00:42.417 --> 00:00:44.627
were often described
as beautiful maidens

00:00:44.711 --> 00:00:47.297
decked out in battle gear
and armed to the teeth

00:00:47.380 --> 00:00:48.965
with brutal weapons.

00:00:49.048 --> 00:00:50.550
Using their powers
of foresight

00:00:50.633 --> 00:00:53.136
and mystical abilities
to sway the tides of war,

00:00:53.219 --> 00:00:56.973
the Valkyries were said to fly
over battlefields on horses,

00:00:57.056 --> 00:00:59.517
or sometimes they would
sport their own wings,

00:00:59.601 --> 00:01:00.977
like the goddess Freyja,

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:03.146
who may have been
the first Valkyrie.

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:05.315
The Valkyries
were at least as deadly

00:01:05.398 --> 00:01:08.067
as they were beautiful,
and were said to be blessed

00:01:08.151 --> 00:01:10.320
with superhuman strength
and speed.

00:01:10.403 --> 00:01:11.738
In some stories,

00:01:11.821 --> 00:01:14.115
they could even shapeshift
into different birds,

00:01:14.199 --> 00:01:15.784
Most Valkyries went unnamed,

00:01:15.867 --> 00:01:18.620
but those who weren't
were given war-themed monikers

00:01:18.703 --> 00:01:21.456
like Skogul,
Sigrún, or Hlokk,

00:01:21.539 --> 00:01:23.333
which mean "shaker,"
"victory rune,"

00:01:23.416 --> 00:01:25.001
and "clashing battle."

00:01:25.084 --> 00:01:27.670
The word Valkyrie means
"chooser of the slain,"

00:01:27.754 --> 00:01:29.255
and they were indeed
as selective

00:01:29.339 --> 00:01:30.757
as their name implies.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:33.343
Only half of the Norse warriors
who died in battle

00:01:33.426 --> 00:01:35.845
were carried by the Valkyries
to Valhalla.

00:01:35.929 --> 00:01:38.139
The rest were spirited away
to Fólkvangr

00:01:38.223 --> 00:01:40.433
to spend their afterlives
with Freyja.

00:01:40.517 --> 00:01:42.477
Once in Valhalla,
the dead heroes,

00:01:42.560 --> 00:01:44.354
or einherjar
as they were called,

00:01:44.437 --> 00:01:47.315
would spend their days
fighting and nights feasting,

00:01:47.398 --> 00:01:50.610
preparing to defend Asgard
in the world's last battle.

00:01:50.693 --> 00:01:53.530
Despite the power they
wielded on the battlefield,

00:01:53.613 --> 00:01:55.406
the Valkyries' role
was relegated

00:01:55.490 --> 00:01:57.575
to that of servant
in Odin's hall.

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:00.328
The mead that the einherjar
drank to heal their wounds

00:02:00.411 --> 00:02:02.747
from training was served
by the very same hands

00:02:02.831 --> 00:02:04.874
that plucked them away
from Midgard.

00:02:04.958 --> 00:02:07.669
In addition to deciding
individual warriors' fates,

00:02:07.752 --> 00:02:10.046
the Valkyries were
sometimes said to determine

00:02:10.129 --> 00:02:12.215
the outcome of entire battles.

00:02:12.298 --> 00:02:14.717
An old Norse poem
called the Hákonarmál

00:02:14.801 --> 00:02:17.387
recounts how two Valkyries,
named Skogul and Gondul,

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:19.222
influenced
the Battle of Fitjar,

00:02:19.305 --> 00:02:22.058
fought in Norway in 961 CE.

00:02:22.141 --> 00:02:23.893
King Hákon the Good
faced an army

00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:26.938
led by his nephews
who wanted to take his throne.

00:02:27.021 --> 00:02:28.648
The poet who
immortalized the clash

00:02:28.731 --> 00:02:30.984
wrote that Hákon
tore off his mailsuit

00:02:31.067 --> 00:02:32.277
and, with his sword,

00:02:32.360 --> 00:02:33.945
hacked through
his opponents' armor

00:02:34.028 --> 00:02:36.030
"as if it were
cutting through water."

00:02:36.114 --> 00:02:38.074
The would-be usurpers
were defeated,

00:02:38.157 --> 00:02:40.577
but King Hákon
suffered mortal injuries,

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:43.246
and when the Valkyries
appeared to take him away,

00:02:43.329 --> 00:02:45.915
he asked Skogul and Gondul
why they came for him

00:02:45.999 --> 00:02:48.835
when he fought so well
and defeated his nephews.

00:02:48.918 --> 00:02:51.337
The Valkyries replied
that it was only because

00:02:51.421 --> 00:02:54.048
they were with him in battle
that he was able

00:02:54.132 --> 00:02:56.426
to hold the field,
and his enemies fled.

00:02:56.509 --> 00:02:58.261
Way to kick a man
while he's down.

00:02:58.344 --> 00:03:01.598
In their capacity to predict
and sway the outcome of war,

00:03:01.681 --> 00:03:04.642
the Valkyries were similar
to Norns, deities of destiny

00:03:04.726 --> 00:03:06.853
who you may remember
from our episode

00:03:06.936 --> 00:03:08.313
on the Greek Fates.

00:03:08.396 --> 00:03:11.107
A poem found in Njál's Saga
from the 13th century

00:03:11.190 --> 00:03:14.444
makes this comparison
abundantly clear

00:03:14.527 --> 00:03:15.695
when the titular Daurrud

00:03:15.778 --> 00:03:17.488
happened upon a group
of Valkyries

00:03:17.572 --> 00:03:20.158
weaving the fate
of a battle later that day.

00:03:20.241 --> 00:03:23.786
Daurrud spied 12 Valkyries
working around a gruesome loom

00:03:23.870 --> 00:03:27.332
with human heads for weights
and swords for shuttles.

00:03:27.415 --> 00:03:29.626
The thread was made
from human entrails,

00:03:29.709 --> 00:03:31.544
and the Valkyries
sang while they wove

00:03:31.628 --> 00:03:33.463
their "war-winning woof."

00:03:33.546 --> 00:03:35.798
The Norns weren't
the only mythical figures

00:03:35.882 --> 00:03:37.383
who blended
with the Valkyries.

00:03:37.467 --> 00:03:38.968
By the time the Eddas
were written,

00:03:39.052 --> 00:03:40.970
the Valkyries had taken on
characteristics

00:03:41.054 --> 00:03:42.680
of the German swan-maiden.

00:03:42.764 --> 00:03:44.682
Like Selkies
from Celtic folklore,

00:03:44.766 --> 00:03:47.602
swan-maidens were able
to shift from their animal form

00:03:47.685 --> 00:03:49.145
into beautiful women.

00:03:49.228 --> 00:03:51.648
Their good looks were
often their downfall, however,

00:03:51.731 --> 00:03:53.274
as was common in stories

00:03:53.358 --> 00:03:55.652
for men to steal
a maiden's swan suit.

00:03:55.735 --> 00:03:58.821
And without her feathers,
she was stuck in human form.

00:03:58.905 --> 00:04:01.991
Even Brynhildr, one
of the most famous Valkyries,

00:04:02.075 --> 00:04:03.910
fell prey to this avian theft.

00:04:03.993 --> 00:04:06.829
While the man involved suffered
very little consequence,

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:09.791
of course, Brynhildr
went on to live a life

00:04:09.874 --> 00:04:12.418
of punishment
for his foul deed.

00:04:12.502 --> 00:04:14.420
According to
the Volsunga Saga,

00:04:14.504 --> 00:04:17.298
Brynhildr was the
human daughter of King Budli,

00:04:17.382 --> 00:04:19.133
lovely enough that Odin
picked her

00:04:19.217 --> 00:04:21.177
to be one of his Valkyries.

00:04:21.260 --> 00:04:22.679
One day, Brynhildr
and her sisters

00:04:22.762 --> 00:04:25.348
were out bathing in a pool
with their swan suits

00:04:25.431 --> 00:04:27.600
tucked away
on the bank to stay dry.

00:04:27.684 --> 00:04:29.060
Unbeknownst
to the young Valkyries,

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:31.104
a prince named Agnar
was hiding,

00:04:31.187 --> 00:04:32.939
waiting for them
to be distracted

00:04:33.022 --> 00:04:35.692
so he could steal
the maidens' feather suits.

00:04:35.775 --> 00:04:39.362
Brynhildr and her sisters were
stuck with Agnar for years,

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:41.531
and whether through coercion,
real love,

00:04:41.614 --> 00:04:43.616
or some twisted combination
of the two,

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:47.453
Brynhildr ended up making
an oath of fealty to Agnar.

00:04:47.537 --> 00:04:50.081
Later, after he returned
Brynhildr's feathers,

00:04:50.164 --> 00:04:52.458
Agnar fought
against Hjalmgunnar,

00:04:52.542 --> 00:04:54.836
King of the Goths,
who was favored by Odin

00:04:54.919 --> 00:04:56.212
to win the battle.

00:04:56.295 --> 00:04:58.089
But Brynhildr used
her Valkyrie powers

00:04:58.172 --> 00:05:01.092
to uphold her oath
and sway the outcome.

00:05:01.175 --> 00:05:03.845
Agnar emerged
a victorious underdog,

00:05:03.928 --> 00:05:06.055
and Odin was furious.

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:08.224
His punishment
for Brynhildr's betrayal

00:05:08.307 --> 00:05:10.893
was to banish her
to a fully mortal life,

00:05:10.977 --> 00:05:14.397
slumbering in a castle,
guarded by a wall of fire.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:16.733
An early sleeping beauty,
if you will.

00:05:16.816 --> 00:05:18.901
Maybe you can guess
what happens next.

00:05:18.985 --> 00:05:20.403
A hero named Sigurd,

00:05:20.486 --> 00:05:22.405
aka Seigfried
the Dragon Slayer,

00:05:22.488 --> 00:05:24.282
was brave enough
to pass through the flames

00:05:24.365 --> 00:05:26.659
and immediately
fell in love with Brynhildr,

00:05:26.743 --> 00:05:29.912
but left her behind while
he ran an errand in Bergundy.

00:05:29.996 --> 00:05:32.123
While there, he met
a jealous sorceress

00:05:32.206 --> 00:05:34.083
who wanted him to marry
her daughter,

00:05:34.167 --> 00:05:37.128
so she cast a spell on him
and sent her son Gunnar

00:05:37.211 --> 00:05:39.047
to marry Brynhildr instead.

00:05:39.130 --> 00:05:41.007
Except Gunnar
was too much of a coward

00:05:41.090 --> 00:05:42.842
to walk through
her protective fire,

00:05:42.925 --> 00:05:45.636
and Sigurdr had to do it
disguised as Gunnar.

00:05:45.720 --> 00:05:48.181
Brynhildr married Gunnar
based on that lie,

00:05:48.264 --> 00:05:49.766
and when she found out
the truth,

00:05:49.849 --> 00:05:51.893
she arranged
for Sigurdr to be murdered

00:05:51.976 --> 00:05:54.479
and then threw herself
onto his funeral pyre.

00:05:54.562 --> 00:05:56.481
I swear, modern-day
soap operas

00:05:56.564 --> 00:05:58.816
should dip into Norse folklore
for inspiration.

00:05:58.900 --> 00:06:01.778
Brynhildr's fascinating,
albeit tragic, life

00:06:01.861 --> 00:06:03.071
illustrates the mystery

00:06:03.154 --> 00:06:04.947
surrounding
the mythical Valkyrie.

00:06:05.031 --> 00:06:08.034
She was a human woman
trained to be a shield-maiden,

00:06:08.117 --> 00:06:09.619
granted divinity by Odin,

00:06:09.702 --> 00:06:13.372
who made herself vulnerable
by shifting into a swan.

00:06:13.456 --> 00:06:15.333
Scholars believe
that the basic idea

00:06:15.416 --> 00:06:18.127
of a Scandinavian war goddess
is quite old,

00:06:18.211 --> 00:06:20.046
but that these varying
characteristics

00:06:20.129 --> 00:06:22.381
were added to the mythology
over time.

00:06:22.465 --> 00:06:24.258
They all would have been
written down together

00:06:24.342 --> 00:06:26.761
when the Eddas were composed
in the 13th century.

00:06:26.844 --> 00:06:28.179
But that begs the question:

00:06:28.262 --> 00:06:30.389
where did the Valkyries
come from?

00:06:30.473 --> 00:06:32.725
One hypothesis is that
the legend arose

00:06:32.809 --> 00:06:34.685
from the totally mundane
phenomenon

00:06:34.769 --> 00:06:36.687
of the Norse shield-maiden.

00:06:36.771 --> 00:06:37.939
These were warrior women

00:06:38.022 --> 00:06:40.483
who fought alongside
male Vikings in battle.

00:06:40.566 --> 00:06:42.944
Modern scientists
have used DNA evidence

00:06:43.027 --> 00:06:46.322
to confirm that Norse women
did indeed fight with men,

00:06:46.405 --> 00:06:48.699
and it's not too difficult
to see how this practice

00:06:48.783 --> 00:06:51.160
might lead to myths
about ferocious women

00:06:51.244 --> 00:06:53.121
who decided the fate of war.

00:06:53.204 --> 00:06:55.289
It would also explain
why so many Valkyries

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:57.083
are said
to have human parents.

00:06:57.166 --> 00:06:59.293
Another idea is
that the stories were inspired

00:06:59.377 --> 00:07:02.255
by priestesses of Odin
in the early Viking Age.

00:07:02.338 --> 00:07:05.049
These women would have
overseen sacrificial rituals

00:07:05.133 --> 00:07:07.510
in Odin's honor,
literally choosing

00:07:07.593 --> 00:07:09.595
who would be slain
in his name.

00:07:09.679 --> 00:07:11.097
This sounds familiar,

00:07:11.180 --> 00:07:13.182
and it would explain
why Valkyries were described

00:07:13.266 --> 00:07:15.810
as the physical manifestation
of Odin's will

00:07:15.893 --> 00:07:18.688
in the older mythological poems
of the Eddas.

00:07:18.771 --> 00:07:21.107
We may never know
the Valkyries' true origins,

00:07:21.190 --> 00:07:23.401
but we can say for certain
that they have made

00:07:23.484 --> 00:07:25.862
a large impact
on our popular culture.

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:28.573
Valkyries can be found
in comics, anime,

00:07:28.656 --> 00:07:30.408
television,
and video games.

00:07:30.491 --> 00:07:32.827
In the 1850s,
Richard Wagner composed

00:07:32.910 --> 00:07:35.872
his opera Die Walküre,
or The Valkyries,

00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:38.207
which was one
of his four music dramas

00:07:38.291 --> 00:07:40.418
inspired by Norse mythology.

00:07:40.501 --> 00:07:42.670
This one loosely follows
Brynhildr's story

00:07:42.753 --> 00:07:44.839
and features the absurdly
catchy tune,

00:07:44.922 --> 00:07:46.757
"The Ride of the Valkyries."

00:07:46.841 --> 00:07:49.886
And of course, we can see
Valkyrie on the silver screen,

00:07:49.969 --> 00:07:53.055
played by Tessa Thompson
in Marvel's "Thor" franchise.

00:07:53.139 --> 00:07:55.892
The Valkyrie is depicted
as a strong, powerful woman

00:07:55.975 --> 00:07:59.020
with influence over life
and death, even today.

00:07:59.103 --> 00:08:01.647
It may seem strange
that a culture so infamous

00:08:01.731 --> 00:08:03.065
for its physical dominance

00:08:03.149 --> 00:08:05.109
would hold women
in such high esteem.

00:08:05.193 --> 00:08:07.737
But women had more rights
in Scandinavian culture

00:08:07.820 --> 00:08:08.988
than you might think.

00:08:09.071 --> 00:08:10.615
Their gender roles
were typical,

00:08:10.698 --> 00:08:12.408
with men doing most
of the physical labor

00:08:12.491 --> 00:08:14.160
and women taking care
of the home,

00:08:14.243 --> 00:08:16.996
but women could own property,
divorce their husbands,

00:08:17.079 --> 00:08:18.414
and often had a say

00:08:18.497 --> 00:08:20.458
in who their parents
married them off to.

00:08:20.541 --> 00:08:23.628
Naturally, people with
so many fierce female figures

00:08:23.711 --> 00:08:26.672
in their mythology would
have respect for mortal women.

00:08:26.756 --> 00:08:28.925
We clearly haven't forgotten
the Valkyrie,

00:08:29.008 --> 00:08:32.178
so let's not forget
this lesson they teach us.

00:08:34.847 --> 00:08:37.892
Accessibility provided by the
U.S. Department of Education.

